1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,600
Nasal dryness, up to 65 percent of people using a CPAP machine report it, and it is not just from the airflow.

2
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:16,240
The lining inside your nose needs constant moisture to stay flexible, and when it dries out it cracks, crusts, and bleeds easily.

3
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:21,680
CPAP therapy, frequent flying, and forced-air home heating all speed that up.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,280
First is CPAP therapy without enough humidification.

5
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:30,160
The constant pressurized airflow strips moisture from the nasal lining all night.

6
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:36,160
Turn on your machine's heated humidifier and raise the setting if your nose still feels dry by morning.

7
00:00:36,480 --> 00:00:38,720
Second is frequent air travel.

8
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:46,000
Airplane cabin air holds as little as ten to twenty percent humidity, far drier than any room on the ground.

9
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:52,400
Use a saline nasal spray before boarding and again during long flights to keep the lining moist.

10
00:00:52,720 --> 00:00:54,880
Third is forced-air furnace heating.

11
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:04,880
Winter indoor humidity in homes with forced-air heat can drop to fifteen to twenty-five percent, well below the thirty to fifty percent nasal tissue needs.

12
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,360
Run a humidifier near your bed so the moisture actually reaches you.

13
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:18,320
Humidifying your CPAP, spraying saline before flights, and running a bedroom humidifier all season make a real difference for nasal dryness.
